[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ The gambit position: his pawn stands on d4, your bishop on c4 stares at f7, and your king has not castled. }
4... Bb4+ { He checks you straight away from b4, before doing anything else. How should you deal with this early check? } 5. c3 { Good — block the check with your pawn, and in the same move offer him a trade right in the middle of the board. [%cal Gc3d4] } (5. Bd2 { Blocking the check with your own bishop instead is perfectly fine too, and it leads to a calm, simple position. } 5... Bxd2+ { The bishops come off and the game is calm. The pawn block fights for more. }) (5. Nbd2 { Blocking with the queen knight is playable, but it clogs up your own pieces and slows your development. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O O-O 7. e5 Ng4 { He settles into a perfectly comfortable position, with nothing of yours actively troubling him. }) (5. Kf1 { Moving your king to block the check throws away your right to castle for the rest of the game. } 5... Nf6 6. a3 Bc5 7. b4 Be7 { You end up worse off here for no good reason, having given up castling for nothing. }) 5... dxc3 { He grabs the pawn on c3. Two different pieces of yours could recapture it — which one is actually the right choice? } 6. bxc3 { The b pawn takes back and guards d4. The b column is open now, so your rook can swing from a1 to b1. [%cal Ga1b1,Gb1b7,Gc3d4] } (6. Nxc3 { Recapturing with the knight is also perfectly good — it develops a new piece at the very moment it captures. } 6... d6 { Also good: a piece comes out as it captures. The pawn keeps d4 guarded. }) (6. O-O { Castling straight away leaves the pawn alone entirely, and simply lets him keep it in his pocket for now. } 6... Nf6 { Playable, but he keeps the pawn on c3 for now. Taking at once is cleaner. }) (6. Kf1 { Never move your king here instead of recapturing — he simply grabs yet another pawn on his very next move. } 6... cxb2 7. Bxb2 Nf6 8. e5 Nh5 { You end up two whole pawns down, and your own rook in the corner has nowhere useful left to go. }) 6... Ba5 { His bishop slides back to a5 to stay on the board. Your own king, meanwhile, is still standing in the middle. } 7. O-O { Your king is safe on g1, your bishop still stares at f7, and your pawns on c3 and e4 hold the centre. A pawn down, and every piece ready. [%cal Gc4f7] } (7. Qb3 { Doubling up on f7 with the queen looks tempting and sharp, but he simply defends the pawn with ease. } 7... Qf6 { His queen on f6 guards f7 with ease, and your queen is out early as a target. [%cal Rf6f7] }) (7. Ba3 { Stopping him from castling with this bishop move looks clever, but the bishop itself ends up stranded and useless. } 7... Nge7 8. Ng5 d5 9. exd5 Ne5 { He calmly unwinds his position over the next few moves, and you are left with the worse side of the game. }) (7. Nbd2 { The knight settles onto a passive square here, doing very little for the rest of the game. } 7... Nge7 8. O-O h6 9. Bb3 O-O { This works out fine for him, and your own knight is left with no real future anywhere on the board. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: the quiet d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/89PLb4a2/WfUCmwvV"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 1 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/2BpP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_1_4"]

{ The gambit position: his pawn stands on d4, and nothing of his is out yet except the knight on c6. }
4... d6 { A quiet little pawn move to d6, played purely to defend the extra pawn on d4 for now. How should you answer it? } 5. Nxd4 { Simple and strong — since he never grabbed for more, there is no need for a gambit here. Just take the pawn straight back. [%csl Gd4] } (5. c3 { You can offer a second pawn instead. He takes it, you develop fast — but nothing makes him give anything back. } 5... dxc3 6. Nxc3 Nf6 7. Qb3 Qd7 { Lively and playable, though he keeps the extra pawn and you are the one who has to prove something. }) (5. O-O { Castling first here is also good, and every bit as playable as recapturing the pawn immediately. } 5... Bg4 { Also good. Taking the pawn back at once is simply more straightforward. }) (5. Bb5 { Pinning the knight this way is playable, but it usually ends in trading off your best attacking bishop for it. } 5... Bd7 6. O-O Be7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 { Material stays equal here, and along with the bishop trade, most of the fun in the position has gone too. }) (5. Ng5 { The familiar lunge toward f7 simply does not work in this position — he guards the square with ease. } 5... Nh6 6. Nf3 Qf6 7. Bb5 Qg6 { Your knight has spent two whole moves going back and forth, and there is nothing to show for the trip. }) 5... Nf6 { His knight comes out to f6, putting a little pressure on your e pawn. Your own king, though, is still sitting in the middle. } 6. O-O { Well done — king safe first. And the pawn on e4 is not really hanging: if his knight grabs it, your rook swings to e1 and pins it against his king. [%cal Gf1e1] } (6. Nc3 { Developing the last knight here is also good, and it guards the e pawn as a useful side effect. } 6... Be7 { Also good: the knight guards e4 too. Either move is fine here. [%cal Gc3e4] }) (6. Nxc6 { Trading knights here mostly helps him — his b pawn gets to recapture toward the middle of the board. } 6... bxc6 7. Nc3 Be7 8. b3 a5 { This position is still fine for you, but the trade has given away all the tension that made it interesting. }) (6. Bg5 { Pinning the knight here achieves very little — he simply breaks the pin himself on the very next move. } 6... h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Nb5 Qd8 { You have ended up trading a genuinely good bishop for one of his knights, which is no bargain at all. }) 6... Be7 { He tucks his bishop quietly onto e7, ready to castle next move. Notice which one of your own pieces is still missing. } 7. Nc3 { Perfect — the last knight comes out and guards your pawn on e4. Both knights and the bishop are working, your king is safe, and the middlegame can start. [%csl Gc3] } (7. Nxc6 { Trading knights again here just repeats the earlier idea — his b pawn marches to the middle and helps his position. } 7... bxc6 8. Nc3 a5 9. b3 O-O { This stays perfectly fine for you, but once again you have helped his pawn structure along for no reason. }) (7. Re1 { Bringing the rook to the centre is a useful move in itself, but developing the last knight really ought to come first. } 7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 { Still good for you, a touch slower than bringing out the last knight. }) (7. Bb5 { Pinning the knight this way gives away your best attacking bishop yet again, just as it did earlier in this line. } 7... Bd7 8. Nxc6 Bxc6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Material stays equal here, and once more the attacking bishop that made your position dangerous has disappeared. }) 1-0


[Event "Scotch Gambit: he takes with the knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
